package com.hpcai270

// Days 152–168: InfiniBand, RDMA, RoCE and high-speed fabric engineering.
val hpcDaysPart15 = listOf(
Day(152,"InfiniBand architecture","The HPC fabric from HCA to switch","InfiniBand is a purpose-built high-performance interconnect. Learn HCAs, ports, links, switches, subnet management, queue pairs and the fabric control plane.","PATH\n\nProcess → RDMA verbs → HCA → InfiniBand link → switch fabric → destination HCA → memory.\n\nStorage can use the same fabric as compute or a dedicated storage fabric.","COMPONENTS\n\nHCA, switch, subnet manager, GUID, LID, port and link state. Separate control plane from data plane.","AI\n\nCollectives and storage benefit from predictable low-latency, high-bandwidth paths. The fabric must be engineered as a system.","LAB\n\nDraw a 4-node leaf-spine InfiniBand fabric. Label HCA ports, switches and subnet-manager roles.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy can an AI cluster need a separate storage fabric? Discuss isolation, predictable bandwidth and failure-domain control.","GOAL\n\nSee InfiniBand as an engineered fabric, not simply a faster Ethernet cable."),
Day(153,"RDMA fundamentals","Remote memory access without normal CPU copy paths","RDMA allows an application to move data between hosts using network adapters with reduced CPU involvement compared with traditional socket I/O.","CONCEPTS\n\nQueue Pair, Completion Queue, Work Request, Work Completion, Memory Region and protection domain.","OPERATIONS\n\nMemory must be registered and permissions matter. RDMA is fast because work is offloaded toward the NIC; it does not remove topology or congestion limits.","LATENCY\n\nCPU overhead, PCIe topology, NIC processing, switch hops and congestion all contribute.","LAB\n\nUse perftest or equivalent in a controlled environment when available. Compare message size and bandwidth/latency behavior.","INTERVIEW\n\nExplain RDMA read/write versus send/receive at a conceptual level.","GOAL\n\nUnderstand what RDMA changes in the data path."),
Day(154,"Queue pairs and completion queues","How RDMA work is submitted","Queue pairs contain work queues; completion queues report completed operations. These structures are central to RDMA performance.","MODEL\n\nApplication posts work request → HCA executes → remote operation/message → completion generated → application handles completion.","SCALING\n\nToo few queues can limit concurrency; too many consume resources. Queue affinity and CPU/NIC locality matter.","TROUBLESHOOTING\n\nA connection may exist while throughput is low because of queue depth, message size, CPU polling or congestion.","LAB\n\nDraw 8 worker threads and 8 QPs feeding one HCA. Explain how concurrency changes throughput.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat is a QP and why does an HPC storage engineer need to understand it?", "GOAL\n\nMove from ‘RDMA is fast’ to understanding the mechanism."),
Day(155,"RoCEv2","RDMA over Ethernet","RoCEv2 carries RDMA traffic over Ethernet/IP. The Ethernet fabric must be engineered for low loss and controlled congestion.","STACK\n\nApplication → RDMA verbs → NIC → RoCEv2/UDP/IP → Ethernet → switch → destination NIC.","FABRIC\n\nECN, PFC, QoS, MTU and correct configuration are important depending on design. Misconfiguration can create severe performance problems.","TRADE-OFF\n\nRoCE can reuse Ethernet infrastructure, but predictable RDMA performance requires disciplined configuration.","LAB\n\nCompare an Ethernet TCP path with a RoCE path. List additional fabric controls.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy is PFC not a universal cure for congestion? Discuss pause propagation and ECN.","GOAL\n\nUnderstand RoCE as a system-level networking discipline."),
Day(156,"Congestion control","Why fast networks still become slow","At high scale, congestion is often the performance limiter. AI clusters create synchronized bursts from collectives, checkpoints and dataset reads.","MODEL\n\nMany senders → shared link → queue grows → latency rises → drops or pause/ECN behavior → throughput changes.","SIGNALS\n\nECN marks, drops, pause counters, queue occupancy, retransmissions and application latency.","AI\n\nA synchronized workload can create incast: many nodes send toward fewer destinations. Storage reads can create similar hotspots.","LAB\n\nDraw a 16-to-1 incast scenario. Identify where congestion forms and what metrics prove it.","INTERVIEW\n\nExplain why a 400/800 Gb/s link can still deliver poor application throughput.","GOAL\n\nDiagnose congestion from evidence."),
Day(157,"PFC and ECN","RoCE control mechanisms","PFC can pause selected traffic classes; ECN marks congestion so endpoints can react. They solve different parts of congestion behavior.","PFC\n\nPause propagation can protect a class but may create head-of-line blocking or pause storms if poorly designed.","ECN\n\nSwitches mark congestion; endpoints react according to congestion-control algorithms.","DESIGN\n\nQoS class → priority → switch queues → PFC/ECN policy → endpoint behavior. Consistency across the path matters.","LAB\n\nCreate a fabric policy diagram and list what must match between NICs and switches.","INTERVIEW\n\nIf PFC pause time rises and throughput falls, what hypotheses do you test first?", "GOAL\n\nTreat QoS as an end-to-end contract."),
Day(158,"InfiniBand subnet management","How the fabric becomes usable","The subnet manager discovers and configures the InfiniBand fabric, providing addressing and routes needed for communication.","CONTROL PLANE\n\nDiscovery → topology knowledge → addressing → routing → link activation.","OPERATIONS\n\nUnderstand manager health, fabric state and link state. A link can be physically up while the fabric is not correctly configured.","TOOLS\n\nibstat, ibv_devinfo, iblinkinfo, ibnetdiscover and vendor tools are common starting points.","LAB\n\nStudy sample topology output and translate it into a physical diagram.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat happens if the subnet manager is unavailable? Distinguish existing data paths from new/reconfigured fabric behavior.","GOAL\n\nUnderstand the control plane that makes IB forwarding possible."),
Day(159,"Fabric topology","Leaf-spine, fat-tree and oversubscription","Topology determines bisection bandwidth, path diversity and failure behavior. Balanced fabrics reduce synchronized workload bottlenecks.","FAT-TREE\n\nLeaf/edge switches connect nodes; spine layers provide equal-cost paths. A balanced design minimizes oversubscription.","OVERSUBSCRIPTION\n\nIf many downlinks share less uplink capacity, the ratio predicts potential contention.","AI\n\nCollectives stress east-west bandwidth. Storage traffic adds another dimension unless fabrics are separated.","LAB\n\nCalculate oversubscription for a simple 32-node leaf with 32×400G downlinks and 8×400G uplinks.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy can a non-blocking fabric matter more for AI than ordinary enterprise traffic?", "GOAL\n\nDesign fabrics from traffic patterns."),
Day(160,"NIC/HCA topology and GPU locality","PCIe and fabric locality","High-speed networking performance depends on which PCIe root complex and CPU socket owns a NIC and which GPU is close to which fabric rail.","TOPOLOGY\n\nCPU socket ↔ PCIe root ↔ NIC/HCA ↔ fabric; GPU ↔ PCIe/NVLink. The shortest path often matters.","TOOLS\n\nlspci -tv, numactl, nvidia-smi topo -m and vendor topology tools.","AI\n\nA GPU using a remote NUMA NIC may consume additional PCIe/inter-socket resources. Rail-optimized designs balance paths.","LAB\n\nDraw a dual-socket 8-GPU node and label two NICs. Decide which NIC each GPU should prefer.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy can identical NICs have different application performance depending on placement?", "GOAL\n\nInclude PCIe topology in performance reasoning."),
Day(161,"Network benchmarking","iperf is not enough","Network testing must match the transport and workload. TCP bandwidth, RDMA bandwidth, latency and application traffic answer different questions.","TEST LAYERS\n\nTCP/UDP baseline → RDMA microbenchmark → filesystem/application benchmark.","VARIABLES\n\nMessage size, concurrency, streams, direction, CPU affinity, MTU, topology and traffic class.","LAB\n\nDesign a benchmark matrix for 2, 8 and 32 nodes. Record aggregate and per-node throughput and tail latency.","INTERVIEW\n\nA NIC reports 400 Gb/s but application throughput is 250 Gb/s. What evidence do you collect before blaming the NIC?", "GOAL\n\nBenchmark the layer where the claim exists."),
Day(162,"RDMA troubleshooting","A disciplined fault tree","RDMA failures span hardware, firmware, driver, kernel, userspace and fabric configuration.","FAULT TREE\n\nPCIe device → driver → firmware → RDMA device → link → path → QP setup → application → performance.","TOOLS\n\nrdma link, ibstat, ibv_devinfo, ethtool, dmesg, vendor firmware tools, switch telemetry and perftest.","SYMPTOMS\n\nNo device, link down, connection failure, low bandwidth, high latency or intermittent completion errors.","LAB\n\nCreate a troubleshooting matrix with evidence for five RDMA symptoms.","INTERVIEW\n\nHow do you distinguish a fabric problem from an application/QP problem? Compare measurements.","GOAL\n\nTroubleshoot RDMA from bottom to top."),
Day(163,"NVMe over Fabrics","Remote NVMe command path","NVMe-oF extends the NVMe command model to remote storage. Common transports include RDMA and TCP.","PATH\n\nApplication → block layer → NVMe host → NVMe-oF transport → fabric → NVMe target → SSDs.","RDMA VERSION\n\nCan reduce software/network overhead and is common in high-performance designs. TCP offers broader Ethernet compatibility.","AI\n\nNVMe-oF can expose high-performance remote flash, while distributed filesystems are used when many clients need shared POSIX semantics.","LAB\n\nCompare local NVMe, NVMe-oF/RDMA and NVMe-oF/TCP for latency, CPU overhead and operational complexity.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhen would you choose NVMe-oF instead of a parallel filesystem? Discuss block versus shared-file semantics.","GOAL\n\nChoose storage protocol from application semantics."),
Day(164,"Fabric isolation","Compute, storage and management networks","Large AI systems often separate traffic classes or fabrics to protect performance and operations.","EXAMPLE\n\nCompute fabric: GPU-to-GPU. Storage fabric: node-to-storage. Management: provisioning/monitoring. OOB: BMC/switch management.","WHY\n\nA checkpoint burst should not prevent administrators from reaching nodes. Management traffic should not consume scarce storage bandwidth.","SECURITY\n\nIsolation reduces blast radius but increases infrastructure and operational complexity.","LAB\n\nDesign four network planes for a 128-GPU cluster and specify interfaces, switches and failure domains.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhen is a separate physical storage fabric worth the cost?", "GOAL\n\nTreat network planes as architecture decisions."),
Day(165,"NVIDIA networking ecosystem","ConnectX, Spectrum, Quantum and software","Modern NVIDIA AI infrastructure combines high-speed NICs/DPUs, Ethernet or InfiniBand switches and software for fabric management and telemetry.","REFERENCE\n\nConnectX-class adapters provide high-speed networking/RDMA. Quantum families serve InfiniBand fabrics; Spectrum families serve Ethernet AI fabrics. Exact capabilities depend on generation.","SOFTWARE\n\nUFM and related tooling can provide fabric visibility in supported environments. Network Operator integrates networking into Kubernetes environments.","AI\n\nDo not memorize product names without understanding the traffic path: GPU/node → NIC → switch → NIC → destination.","LAB\n\nBuild a vendor-neutral component map and annotate where NVIDIA products could fit.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat is the difference between a NIC/HCA, DPU and switch? Explain their roles.","GOAL\n\nDiscuss vendor hardware without becoming vendor-dependent."),
Day(166,"Ethernet versus InfiniBand","Choosing the fabric","Both can deliver high performance. The correct decision depends on ecosystem, operational skills, workload, topology, congestion and application support.","COMPARE\n\nInfiniBand is purpose-built for HPC. Ethernet/RoCE uses Ethernet with RDMA. Neither should be selected from headline bandwidth alone.","AI\n\nCollective-heavy training, storage access and multi-tenant traffic place different demands on the fabric.","OPERATIONS\n\nEvaluate monitoring, automation, spares, cabling, support and existing standards.","LAB\n\nWrite a decision matrix for a new 256-GPU cluster. Give weighted scores and document assumptions.","INTERVIEW\n\nIf a company has a mature 400G Ethernet team, why might RoCE be attractive? What must still be proven?", "GOAL\n\nDefend a fabric decision with evidence."),
Day(167,"Network failure domains","Cables, optics, ports, switches and paths","High-speed fabrics fail in many ways: optic, cable, port, switch, line card, power domain, routing and configuration. Resilience must be visible in topology.","DESIGN\n\nDual rails → independent switch paths → redundant power → separate management. Avoid single shared components hidden behind ‘redundant’ labels.","OPERATIONS\n\nTrack link errors, flaps, CRC, optical levels, lane issues, congestion and firmware compatibility.","LAB\n\nTake a 16-node topology and mark what happens if one leaf switch, uplink, NIC and cable fail.","INTERVIEW\n\nDifference between link redundancy and application-level resilience? Explain using storage traffic.","GOAL\n\nDesign for component failure without guessing."),
Day(168,"High-speed networking checkpoint","Whiteboard the fabric","You should now explain TCP/IP basics, RDMA, RoCEv2, InfiniBand, topology, congestion, QoS, NVMe-oF and troubleshooting.","WHITEBOARD\n\nGPU → PCIe → HCA/NIC → leaf → spine → leaf → HCA/NIC → storage or GPU. Add management and monitoring paths.","QUESTIONS\n\nQP? CQ? ECN? PFC? MTU? oversubscription? bisection bandwidth? NUMA locality? rail optimization? NVMe-oF?",
"SCENARIO\n\nStorage throughput drops during a checkpoint. Prove or disprove congestion, backend saturation, client queueing and application synchronization.","LAB\n\nWrite a complete network incident runbook from physical layer to application.","INTERVIEW\n\nExplain why networking is now a core storage skill in AI infrastructure.","STANDARD\n\nYou can sit with a network engineer and discuss the same evidence without hiding behind storage terminology.")
)
